{"networkAttributes":[{"KEGG_PATHWAY_IMAGE":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/pathway/spo/spo00514.png","KEGG_PATHWAY_ORG":"spo","modules_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_ID":"path:spo00514","classes":["Metabolism","Glycan biosynthesis and metabolism"],"description":"O-linked glycosylation is the attachment of monosaccharides to the hydroxyl groups of amino acids, mostly serine and threonine, and is found in eukaryotes, archaea and bacteria. O-glycans exhibit diverse types of modifications where the innermost monosaccharide is N-acetylgalactosamine (map00512), mannose in mammals (map00515), and others (this map) including N-acetylglucosamine, fucose, glucose, galactose, mannose in yeast and arabinose in plants.","diseases":[""],"diseases_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_LINK":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg-bin/show_pathway?spo00514","modules":[""],"name":"Other types of O-glycan biosynthesis [spo00514]_1"}]},
